{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793532623,"nonce":"7j4Ic09SENLVYeHA7GGRRvTZ40o=","title":"zypy: the five Linux CI kernels it loses and where each loses","message":"Linux CI at 1cb0f74c (Xeon 8573C, PyPy 7.3.15, CPython 3.12.3, --fast means): zypy behind the best other runtime on scimark_sor (1.22x PyPy), json_bench (1.18x), raytrace-simple (1.18x), deltablue (CPython's 2.3 ms mean; zypy 16.6) and meteor-contest (1.03x). Reproduced on the NUC (x86_64, PyPy 7.3.20, whose json_bench is 5x slower than 7.3.15, so json is judged against CI's numbers only).\n\nscimark_sor: a single iteration, 271 vs 203 ms on the NUC. Steady state is level: 20000 minus 5000 cycles gives 38.3 us per cycle for zypy, 39.3 for PyPy. The loss is warm-up. The loop enters Cranelift code at 111 ms, small Array2D methods are promoted one by one from 145 ms, the loop's own promotion is requested at 162 ms and its LLVM install takes 46 ms (opt 30), so the loop runs at Cranelift's 65 us per cycle until 208 ms. Levers: request the loop's promotion before its callees', a cheaper LLVM pipeline for the first install, a faster Cranelift loop.\n\nraytrace-simple: steady state 11 to 12 ms vs PyPy 7 to 8.5. Point and Vector fields are run-time numbers (int or float), because some are built from int literals; every arithmetic step computes the int and float results and selects by tag. A copy of the kernel with float literals runs 7.8 to 8.1 ms, level with PyPy. Fixed here: math.sqrt's argument was boxed to convert it to a float (an allocation per intersection test); library calls now convert a number on its parts. raytrace 12.4 to 11.1 ms. The rest needs float-specialised method bodies with a fallback on tag mismatch.\n\nmeteor-contest: steady 34 vs 37 ms, first iteration 97 vs 49 (NUC). On the program thread's critical path (Mac, ZYNTAX_TRACE_LAZY): 27 compiles on the program thread itself (40 ms) and 14 on the first-call thread it waits for (54 ms), led by solve (17 ms: body 9.6, Cranelift 7.7), zb_list_slice_l0 (12 ms, 9 of them HIR optimisation of a library builtin), rotate and flip. The OSR prologue costs 45 ms of Cranelift time over the run (per-header region analysis; 3 to 7 ms for functions with 8 to 13 loop headers, mostly library builtins). Library bodies are optimised and compiled afresh every run; ahead-of-time bodies would take them off the critical path.\n\ndeltablue: steady state 0.4 to 0.9 ms (3x faster than CPython) but the first iteration is 51 to 75 ms of compilation, against CPython's 2.3 ms per iteration.\n\njson_bench: 504 vs 427 ms per iteration on CI; the runtime library makes 44.5M Rust allocations in one run, nearly all while the program runs.\n\nTried and refuted:\n- a faster global allocator for Linux: no candidate moves the CI score by more than 1% without raising peak memory (3bdfe63);\n- value-numbering selects in HIR CSE: Cranelift's egraph already merges duplicate selects; no change;\n- if-converting diamonds whose arms compute a few pure values into selects, run last in the pipeline: SOR unchanged in the LLVM tier, Cranelift-only SOR 3% slower, raytrace slower. Predictable branches beat select chains in Cranelift.","files":null}]}